### 🔴 D-P2-12 — `SharedPreferences::m_last_error` data race

**Status:** `SOURCE_VERIFIED`
**File:** `shared_prefs.cpp` — every `getXxx` method
**Severity:** P2

**Finding:** `m_last_error` is `mutable std::string m_last_error;`. It is written from `const` methods like `getString`:
```cpp
std::string SharedPreferences::getString(
    const std::string& key, const std::string& def_value) const {
    std::shared_lock<std::shared_mutex> lock(m_rw_lock);   // SHARED lock
    ...
    if (it->second.type != PrefType::STRING) {
        m_last_error = "Type mismatch: ...";   // write under shared lock
        return def_value;
    }
}
```
The `shared_lock` allows concurrent readers. Two threads concurrently calling `getInt("k")` on a non-int value will both write to `m_last_error` — **data race** (C++ memory model: UB).

**Fix:** either (a) protect `m_last_error` with its own mutex, (b) drop it entirely and use `std::optional<PrefsError>` return values, or (c) acquire an `unique_lock` on the rare error path only:
```cpp
if (it->second.type != PrefType::STRING) {
    std::unique_lock<std::shared_mutex> wlock(m_rw_lock);   // upgrade
    m_last_error = "Type mismatch: ...";
    return def_value;
}
```
Note: (c) requires re-checking the type after the upgrade (another thread may have changed it) and introduces a second lock acquisition. (a) is cleaner.

**Reproduction:** two threads, same `SharedPreferences`, both calling `getInt("str_key")` where `str_key` is a string value → ASan with `-fsanitize=thread` reports a data race on `m_last_error`.

**Integration:** isolated to `shared_prefs.{h,cpp}`; can be fixed together with D-P2-10 and D-P2-11.

