Value arg_val = get_register(1);        // "First argument after 'this'"
```
The 35c format encodes the register list in `FEDC` (four nibbles) plus a 5th register in `A` (high nibble). The code parses neither — it assumes `this` is `v0` and the first argument is `v1`. R8-minified code routinely places `this` in `v3`, `v5`, `v12`; on any such method the batch interpreter dispatches on garbage.

**Fix (minimal):** parse the actual register list exactly as the `35c` law requires:
```cpp
uint8_t argc = (first_word >> 12) & 0xF;          // AA
uint8_t g    = (first_word >> 8) & 0xF;           // 5th register
uint8_t regs[5] = {
    static_cast<uint8_t>(regs_word & 0xF),        // C
    static_cast<uint8_t>((regs_word >> 4) & 0xF), // D
    static_cast<uint8_t>((regs_word >> 8) & 0xF), // E
    static_cast<uint8_t>((regs_word >> 12) & 0xF),// F
    g
};
std::vector<Value> args;
for (uint8_t i = 0; i < argc && i < 5; ++i)
    args.push_back(get_register(regs[i]));
Value this_obj = args.empty() ? Value::make_null() : args[0];
```
This mirrors the production `execute_invoke_virtual` in `dalvik_engine.cpp` (which already does this correctly).

**Reproduction:** `invoke-virtual {v3, v4}, Lfoo;->bar(Ljava/lang/String;)V` with `this` in `v3`. Current batch reads `v0` (uninitialized) as receiver.

